From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 03:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] util: let the serial thread destroy the fallback util_barrier

util_queue_finish queues one job per queue thread that waits on a
util_barrier, and the job whose util_barrier_wait returns true destroys
the barrier (04c90f292e1, "util/queue: fix a data race detected by TSAN
when finishing the queue", Mesa 22.0). That relies on pthread_barrier_t:
one thread of each round gets PTHREAD_BARRIER_SERIAL_THREAD, and glibc
lets it destroy the barrier right away. macOS (also Haiku and Windows)
uses the mutex + condvar fallback in u_thread.c instead, whose
util_barrier_wait returns true in every thread: every thread destroys
the barrier, the first of them (the thread completing the round) while
the threads it has just released are still waking up in cnd_wait.

On macOS that deadlocks. pthread_mutex_destroy of the unlocked mutex
succeeds; a thread woken in pthread_cond_wait still re-acquires the
destroyed mutex, but its pthread_mutex_unlock then fails with EINVAL
and the mutex stays locked, so the next released thread blocks in
_pthread_mutex_firstfit_lock_slow forever and util_queue_finish waits
for its fence forever. A queue needs 3+ threads for it (2+ released
waiters). KosmicKrisp creates a disk cache per physical device (0055),
whose queue scales up to 4 threads while shaders are stored, and
vkDestroyInstance -> kk_physical_device_destroy -> disk_cache_destroy
runs util_queue_finish. Seen live under virglrenderer (steamac,
2026-10-29, after ~6 game launches in one VM boot): a Venus context's
destroy hung in util_queue_finish -> _util_queue_fence_wait with its
disk$3 thread in util_barrier_wait -> cnd_wait ->
_pthread_mutex_firstfit_lock_slow and disk$0-2 idle; it held the render
server's state lock, so every other context and the guest's virtio-gpu
blob maps stopped with it.

Fix the fallback and keep util_queue as it is: util_barrier_wait returns
true only in the thread that completes the round (as
PTHREAD_BARRIER_SERIAL_THREAD), and util_barrier_destroy first waits
until the threads released by the last round have left
util_barrier_wait, as glibc's pthread_barrier_destroy does. That costs a
counter per round; the extra broadcast only happens while a destroy
waits. Platforms with pthread barriers (Linux, the BSDs) are unchanged.

Upstream state: Mesa main (2026-10-29) has the same fallback. Open MR
!32068 (2024-11, not merged) makes only the serial thread return true;
that alone still lets it destroy the mutex under the released threads,
and the new tests still deadlock with it (below). 74920caa (2026-10-21,
util_queue_finish vs util_queue_kill_threads) is a different deadlock.

Test: src/util/tests/u_queue_test.cpp in util_tests:
util_barrier.one_serial_thread_per_round (4 threads, 1000 rounds),
util_barrier.destroyed_by_serial_thread (util_queue_finish_execute's
pattern on 10000 barriers, 4 threads) and util_queue.finish (a queue at
its 4 threads, 10000 rounds of 4 jobs + util_queue_finish). The two that
can deadlock run in a death test child whose watchdog thread ends it
after 10 s without progress.

Measured on an Apple M2, macOS 27.0.1, -Db_ndebug=true (as build.sh):
  stock         one_serial_thread_per_round 0/3 passed (all 4 threads
                get true), destroyed_by_serial_thread 0/3 and
                util_queue.finish 0/3: deadlocked after 7-551 of 10000
                iterations. A sample of the hung test child is the
                live stack: util_queue_finish -> _util_queue_fence_wait,
                one queue thread in util_queue_finish_execute ->
                util_barrier_wait -> cnd_wait ->
                _pthread_mutex_firstfit_lock_slow, three idle.
  !32068 alone  5/5 passed, 0/5 (deadlocked after 7-1984), 0/5
                (deadlocked after 23-1480).
  this patch    100/100 passed each (0.9 s per repeat of the three).
                The existing util_tests pass 255/255 before and after.
---
 src/util/meson.build            |   1 +
 src/util/tests/u_queue_test.cpp | 194 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 src/util/u_thread.c             |  26 ++++-
 src/util/u_thread.h             |   5 +
 4 files changed, 225 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 create mode 100644 src/util/tests/u_queue_test.cpp

diff --git a/src/util/meson.build b/src/util/meson.build
index 72d8660..c2b05ca 100644
--- a/src/util/meson.build
+++ b/src/util/meson.build
@@ -505,6 +505,7 @@ if with_tests
     'tests/u_memstream_test.cpp',
     'tests/u_printf_test.cpp',
     'tests/u_qsort_test.cpp',
+    'tests/u_queue_test.cpp',
     'tests/u_ycbcr_test.cpp',
     'tests/vector_test.cpp',
   )
diff --git a/src/util/tests/u_queue_test.cpp b/src/util/tests/u_queue_test.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..732c160
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/util/tests/u_queue_test.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,194 @@
+/*
+ * Copyright 2026 steamac contributors
+ * SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+ *
+ * util_barrier and util_queue_finish.
+ *
+ * util_queue_finish queues one job per thread that waits on a barrier, and
+ * the thread that util_barrier_wait returns true to destroys it. That needs
+ * the pthread_barrier_t semantics on every platform: exactly one thread of
+ * each round gets true, and it may destroy the barrier at once while the
+ * other threads are still on their way out of util_barrier_wait.
+ */
+
+#include <stdio.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
+#include <gtest/gtest.h>
+
+#include "c11/threads.h"
+#include "util/os_time.h"
+#include "util/u_atomic.h"
+#include "util/u_queue.h"
+#include "util/u_thread.h"
+
+#define NUM_THREADS 4
+#define NUM_ROUNDS 1000
+#define NUM_ITERATIONS 10000
+
+/* A deadlock would hang the whole test binary, so the workloads that can
+ * deadlock run in a death test child with a watchdog thread that ends it
+ * when the workload makes no progress for that long.
+ */
+#define WATCHDOG_SECONDS 10
+
+static unsigned iterations_done;
+
+static int
+watchdog(void *name)
+{
+   unsigned last = 0, idle = 0;
+
+   while (idle < WATCHDOG_SECONDS) {
+      os_time_sleep(1000000);
+      unsigned done = p_atomic_read(&iterations_done);
+      idle = done == last ? idle + 1 : 0;
+      last = done;
+   }
+   fprintf(stderr, "%s: deadlocked after %u of %u iterations\n",
+           (const char *)name, last, NUM_ITERATIONS);
+   fflush(stderr);
+   _exit(2);
+}
+
+static void
+run_with_watchdog(bool (*workload)(void), const char *name)
+{
+   thrd_t thread;
+   if (thrd_create(&thread, watchdog, (void *)name) != thrd_success)
+      exit(3);
+   exit(workload() ? 0 : 1);
+}
+
+#define EXPECT_NO_DEADLOCK(workload) \
+   EXPECT_EXIT(run_with_watchdog(workload, #workload), \
+               ::testing::ExitedWithCode(0), "")
+
+struct barrier_rounds {
+   util_barrier barrier;
+   unsigned serial[NUM_ROUNDS];
+};
+
+static int
+barrier_rounds_thread(void *data)
+{
+   struct barrier_rounds *t = (struct barrier_rounds *)data;
+
+   for (unsigned i = 0; i < NUM_ROUNDS; i++) {
+      if (util_barrier_wait(&t->barrier))
+         p_atomic_inc(&t->serial[i]);
+   }
+   return 0;
+}
+
+/* pthread_barrier_wait returns PTHREAD_BARRIER_SERIAL_THREAD to one thread
+ * of each round, so util_barrier_wait must return true exactly once.
+ */
+TEST(util_barrier, one_serial_thread_per_round)
+{
+   struct barrier_rounds *t =
+      (struct barrier_rounds *)calloc(1, sizeof(*t));
+   thrd_t threads[NUM_THREADS];
+
+   util_barrier_init(&t->barrier, NUM_THREADS);
+   for (unsigned i = 0; i < NUM_THREADS; i++)
+      ASSERT_EQ(thrd_create(&threads[i], barrier_rounds_thread, t),
+                thrd_success);
+   for (unsigned i = 0; i < NUM_THREADS; i++)
+      thrd_join(threads[i], NULL);
+   util_barrier_destroy(&t->barrier);
+
+   for (unsigned i = 0; i < NUM_ROUNDS; i++)
+      ASSERT_EQ(t->serial[i], 1u) << "round " << i;
+   free(t);
+}
+
+struct barrier_thread {
+   util_barrier *barriers;
+   bool counts_progress;
+};
+
+static int
+barrier_wait_and_destroy_thread(void *data)
+{
+   struct barrier_thread *t = (struct barrier_thread *)data;
+
+   for (unsigned i = 0; i < NUM_ITERATIONS; i++) {
+      /* What util_queue_finish_execute does. */
+      if (util_barrier_wait(&t->barriers[i]))
+         util_barrier_destroy(&t->barriers[i]);
+      if (t->counts_progress)
+         p_atomic_inc(&iterations_done);
+   }
+   return 0;
+}
+
+static bool
+barrier_destroyed_by_serial_thread(void)
+{
+   util_barrier *barriers =
+      (util_barrier *)calloc(NUM_ITERATIONS, sizeof(*barriers));
+   struct barrier_thread args[NUM_THREADS];
+   thrd_t threads[NUM_THREADS];
+
+   if (!barriers)
+      return false;
+   for (unsigned i = 0; i < NUM_ITERATIONS; i++)
+      util_barrier_init(&barriers[i], NUM_THREADS);
+   for (unsigned i = 0; i < NUM_THREADS; i++) {
+      args[i].barriers = barriers;
+      args[i].counts_progress = i == 0;
+      if (thrd_create(&threads[i], barrier_wait_and_destroy_thread,
+                      &args[i]) != thrd_success)
+         return false;
+   }
+   for (unsigned i = 0; i < NUM_THREADS; i++)
+      thrd_join(threads[i], NULL);
+
+   free(barriers);
+   return true;
+}
+
+/* The serial thread destroys the barrier while the threads it released still
+ * re-acquire its mutex inside util_barrier_wait.
+ */
+TEST(util_barrier, destroyed_by_serial_thread)
+{
+   EXPECT_NO_DEADLOCK(barrier_destroyed_by_serial_thread);
+}
+
+static void
+count_job(void *job, void *gdata, int thread_index)
+{
+   p_atomic_inc((int *)job);
+}
+
+static bool
+queue_finish_all_threads(void)
+{
+   struct util_queue queue;
+   int executed = 0;
+
+   if (!util_queue_init(&queue, "test", 32, NUM_THREADS, 0, NULL))
+      return false;
+
+   /* The disk cache's queue once it has scaled up to all of its threads. */
+   util_queue_adjust_num_threads(&queue, NUM_THREADS, false);
+
+   for (unsigned i = 0; i < NUM_ITERATIONS; i++) {
+      for (unsigned j = 0; j < NUM_THREADS; j++)
+         util_queue_add_job(&queue, &executed, NULL, count_job, NULL, 0);
+      util_queue_finish(&queue);
+      if (p_atomic_read(&executed) != (int)((i + 1) * NUM_THREADS))
+         return false;
+      p_atomic_inc(&iterations_done);
+   }
+
+   util_queue_destroy(&queue);
+   return true;
+}
+
+/* disk_cache_destroy -> util_queue_finish on a queue with all its threads. */
+TEST(util_queue, finish)
+{
+   EXPECT_NO_DEADLOCK(queue_finish_all_threads);
+}
diff --git a/src/util/u_thread.c b/src/util/u_thread.c
index d1cf085..c6c4dc4 100644
--- a/src/util/u_thread.c
+++ b/src/util/u_thread.c
@@ -219,6 +219,8 @@ void util_barrier_init(util_barrier *barrier, unsigned count)
 {
    barrier->count = count;
    barrier->waiters = 0;
+   barrier->leaving = 0;
+   barrier->destroying = false;
    barrier->sequence = 0;
    (void) mtx_init(&barrier->mutex, mtx_plain);
    cnd_init(&barrier->condvar);
@@ -226,6 +228,19 @@ void util_barrier_init(util_barrier *barrier, unsigned count)
 
 void util_barrier_destroy(util_barrier *barrier)
 {
+   /* The serial thread may destroy the barrier as soon as util_barrier_wait
+    * returns (util_queue_finish_execute does), as glibc's
+    * pthread_barrier_destroy allows, while the threads it released still
+    * have to re-acquire the mutex in cnd_wait and unlock it. Wait for them:
+    * macOS re-acquires a destroyed mutex in pthread_cond_wait but cannot
+    * unlock it (EINVAL), and the next of them then blocks on it forever.
+    */
+   mtx_lock(&barrier->mutex);
+   barrier->destroying = true;
+   while (barrier->leaving)
+      cnd_wait(&barrier->condvar, &barrier->mutex);
+   mtx_unlock(&barrier->mutex);
+
    assert(barrier->waiters == 0);
    mtx_destroy(&barrier->mutex);
    cnd_destroy(&barrier->condvar);
@@ -233,6 +248,8 @@ void util_barrier_destroy(util_barrier *barrier)
 
 bool util_barrier_wait(util_barrier *barrier)
 {
+   bool serial = false;
+
    mtx_lock(&barrier->mutex);
 
    assert(barrier->waiters < barrier->count);
@@ -244,15 +261,22 @@ bool util_barrier_wait(util_barrier *barrier)
       do {
          cnd_wait(&barrier->condvar, &barrier->mutex);
       } while (sequence == barrier->sequence);
+
+      /* The last released thread out lets util_barrier_destroy go on. */
+      if (--barrier->leaving == 0 && barrier->destroying)
+         cnd_broadcast(&barrier->condvar);
    } else {
+      /* The thread that completes the round is the serial thread. */
+      barrier->leaving += barrier->count - 1;
       barrier->waiters = 0;
       barrier->sequence++;
       cnd_broadcast(&barrier->condvar);
+      serial = true;
    }
 
    mtx_unlock(&barrier->mutex);
 
-   return true;
+   return serial;
 }
 
 #endif
diff --git a/src/util/u_thread.h b/src/util/u_thread.h
index 57ebdc5..82c1239 100644
--- a/src/util/u_thread.h
+++ b/src/util/u_thread.h
@@ -125,6 +125,8 @@ typedef pthread_barrier_t util_barrier;
 typedef struct {
    unsigned count;
    unsigned waiters;
+   unsigned leaving; /* released threads still in util_barrier_wait */
+   bool destroying;
    uint64_t sequence;
    mtx_t mutex;
    cnd_t condvar;
@@ -136,6 +138,9 @@ void util_barrier_init(util_barrier *barrier, unsigned count);
 
 void util_barrier_destroy(util_barrier *barrier);
 
+/* Returns true in exactly one thread of each round (pthread_barrier_wait's
+ * PTHREAD_BARRIER_SERIAL_THREAD), which may destroy the barrier right away.
+ */
 bool util_barrier_wait(util_barrier *barrier);
 
 /*
